/* ============================================================
   ANSWER KEY - Introduction to SQL & Databases (HARD LEVEL)
   Curriculum:   RetailMart V3 - PostgreSQL internals/architecture
   Note: Conceptual/architecture questions - no queries to run.
   ============================================================ */

/* Q1.  MVCC keeps multiple row versions so readers never block writers; UPDATE inserts a new row version and marks the old one dead instead of overwriting in place, so concurrent readers still see a consistent snapshot. */
/* Q2.  xmin/xmax are the transaction IDs that created and (if deleted/updated) deleted a row version; PostgreSQL uses them to decide if a row is visible to a given transaction's snapshot. */
/* Q3.  VACUUM scans for dead tuples (old row versions no longer visible to any transaction) and marks their space reusable; without it, dead tuples pile up because MVCC never overwrites rows in place. */
/* Q4.  VACUUM reclaims space for reuse without locking; VACUUM FULL rewrites the table to shrink it on disk but takes an exclusive lock; VACUUM ANALYZE also refreshes planner statistics - use FULL rarely, on heavily bloated tables during maintenance windows. */
/* Q5.  WAL is an append-only log of every change written before it's applied to data files; it's the foundation of durability because after a crash, PostgreSQL replays the WAL to recover any committed change that hadn't yet reached disk. */
/* Q6.  A checkpoint flushes all dirty (modified) buffer pages to disk and records a point WAL replay can start from; it's triggered periodically by time/WAL volume settings (checkpoint_timeout, max_wal_size). */
/* Q7.  shared_buffers is Postgres's own in-process cache of data pages; the OS page cache is the kernel's separate disk cache - Postgres keeps both because OS cache absorbs reads shared_buffers evicts, avoiding pure reliance on one layer. */
/* Q8.  Parse (check syntax, build parse tree) -> rewrite (apply rules/views) -> plan (optimizer picks an execution strategy) -> execute (run the plan and stream results back to the client). */
/* Q9.  They set the relative cost the planner assigns to a sequential page read vs a random page read, letting the optimizer estimate whether a scan or index lookup will be cheaper on that hardware. */
/* Q10. Logical replication streams decoded row-level changes (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE) that can go cross-version/subset of tables; physical replication streams raw WAL bytes for a byte-identical full-database standby. */
/* Q11. Transaction IDs are finite (32-bit) and wrap around; if autovacuum can't freeze old transaction IDs in time, old rows could appear to vanish or new ones appear "in the future" - Postgres shuts down writes to prevent this. */
/* Q12. HOT lets an UPDATE that doesn't change any indexed column write the new version in the same page and skip updating every index, saving significant write/index-maintenance overhead. */
/* Q13. The visibility map tracks which pages contain only tuples visible to all transactions; index-only scans use it to answer a query straight from the index without touching the heap, when the page is "all visible." */
/* Q14. Autovacuum automatically runs VACUUM/ANALYZE in the background; DBAs sometimes disable it on extremely hot, high-churn tables to avoid autovacuum competing for I/O during peak load, instead scheduling manual vacuums off-peak. */
/* Q15. GEQO (genetic query optimizer) uses a randomized/heuristic search instead of exhaustive join-order enumeration; it kicks in automatically once a query joins more tables than the geqo_threshold (default 12). */
/* Q16. ANALYZE gathers column statistics (value distribution, distinct counts, most-common values, correlation) the planner uses to estimate row counts and choose scan/join strategies - stale stats lead to bad plans. */
/* Q17. pg_stat_statements aggregates execution stats (calls, total/mean time, rows) per normalized query across the whole server, letting you rank and find the slowest or most frequently run queries in production. */
/* Q18. relfilenode is the on-disk file identifier for a relation's data (can change on rewrite/TRUNCATE); the relation OID is the stable catalog identity of the table - they diverge whenever the underlying file is rewritten (e.g. VACUUM FULL, TRUNCATE). */
/* Q19. wal_level controls how much info WAL records: 'minimal' (crash recovery only), 'replica' (adds enough for physical replication/PITR), 'logical' (adds enough for logical decoding/replication). */
/* Q20. Logical decoding extracts row-level changes from WAL into a structured, consumable format (for logical replication/CDC tools); streaming replication just ships raw WAL bytes to build a physical copy. */
/* Q21. synchronous_commit controls how much confirmation a COMMIT waits for (local flush only, vs waiting for a standby to receive/flush/apply WAL) - stricter settings trade latency for stronger durability guarantees across replicas. */
/* Q22. The postmaster is the parent process that listens for connections and forks a new backend process per client connection; each backend then handles that one session's queries independently. */
/* Q23. PostgreSQL periodically checks its lock wait graph for cycles and aborts one transaction to break a detected deadlock; MySQL/InnoDB does similar cycle detection but with different default timeout/victim-selection heuristics. */
/* Q24. Bloat is wasted space from accumulated dead tuples/index entries not yet reclaimed; detect via pgstattuple or pg_stat_user_tables' dead-tuple counts, fix with routine VACUUM (or VACUUM FULL/pg_repack for severe cases). */
/* Q25. It can't - an uncommitted transaction's changes are invisible to other transactions under MVCC; a concurrent SELECT sees the last committed version of that row, not the in-progress insert. */

/* Q26. Dirty read: T2 reads T1's uncommitted change, T1 rolls back - T2 saw data that never existed. Non-repeatable read: T2 reads a row twice, T1 commits an update in between - T2's two reads differ. Phantom read: T2 runs the same range query twice, T1 inserts a matching row in between - a new row "appears." */
/* Q27. PostgreSQL never implements true READ UNCOMMITTED (dirty reads) since MVCC always hides uncommitted data, so it silently upgrades that isolation level request to READ COMMITTED behavior. */
/* Q28. At READ COMMITTED, T2 reads a value, T1 updates+commits it, T2 re-reads and gets a different value (non-repeatable). At REPEATABLE READ, T2's whole transaction uses one fixed snapshot taken at its start, so the second read still returns the original value. */
/* Q29. PostgreSQL's REPEATABLE READ gives each transaction a single consistent snapshot for its whole duration (true snapshot isolation) rather than only guaranteeing individual reads are repeatable as the SQL standard's weaker definition technically requires. */
/* Q30. Under SERIALIZABLE, if two concurrent transactions' combined effect couldn't be reproduced by running them one-after-another in some order, PostgreSQL aborts one with a serialization_failure error at commit time - the app must retry it. */
/* Q31. SSI tracks read/write dependencies between concurrent transactions and aborts one if committing both would violate true serial ordering; it costs more than REPEATABLE READ because it must track these dependencies (predicate locks) across all active transactions rather than just taking a snapshot. */
/* Q32. Write skew: two transactions each read overlapping data, then each write to different rows based on what they read, violating a constraint that spans both rows (e.g. two doctors each check "is someone else on call" and both go off-call); SERIALIZABLE prevents it, REPEATABLE READ does not. */
/* Q33. A lost update happens when two transactions read the same row, both compute a new value, and the second COMMIT silently overwrites the first's change; SELECT FOR UPDATE prevents it by locking the row so the second transaction blocks until the first commits, and SERIALIZABLE prevents it by aborting one transaction at commit. */
/* Q34. FOR UPDATE locks rows against any concurrent update/delete; FOR NO KEY UPDATE is a lighter lock that still allows some concurrent foreign-key-checking operations; FOR SHARE allows concurrent reads but blocks updates; FOR KEY SHARE is the lightest, mainly used for foreign-key checks. */
/* Q35. Row-level locks protect specific existing rows; predicate locks (used by SSI) track the conditions/ranges a transaction read, so it can detect conflicts even with rows that didn't exist yet at read time (needed to catch phantom-style write skew). */
/* Q36. Deadlock: T1 locks row A then waits for row B; T2 locks row B then waits for row A - neither can proceed, so PostgreSQL detects the cycle and aborts one transaction. */
/* Q37. deadlock_timeout is how long a backend waits on a lock before checking for a deadlock cycle; it defaults to 1 second as a balance between quickly detecting real deadlocks and not wasting CPU running the (relatively expensive) deadlock-detection graph check on every normal lock wait. */
/* Q38. Advisory locks are application-defined locks keyed by an arbitrary number, not tied to any row/table; you use them over row locks when coordinating something that isn't a database row at all (e.g. "only one job scheduler instance should run this task"). */
/* Q39. An intent lock signals "I intend to lock at a finer granularity below this level" (used internally in some databases for lock-hierarchy efficiency); PostgreSQL's MVCC design doesn't need this concept exposed at the SQL level since it uses row-level MVCC visibility instead of hierarchical lock escalation. */
/* Q40. Session-level advisory locks persist until explicitly unlocked or the session ends; transaction-level advisory locks are automatically released when the transaction commits or rolls back. */
/* Q41. ACCESS EXCLUSIVE blocks essentially everything on the table - blocks other transactions from SELECT, blocks concurrent INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE, and blocks any other DDL on that table. */
/* Q42. Lock escalation (converting many fine-grained row locks into one coarser table lock to save memory) isn't done in PostgreSQL because its lock manager is efficient enough at row-granularity that escalation's benefit doesn't outweigh the added complexity and blocking risk. */
/* Q43. Many concurrent transactions repeatedly updating the same "hot" row (e.g. a shared counter) serialize on that row's lock, causing contention; design around it by sharding the counter into multiple rows summed on read, or using an append-only log instead of in-place updates. */
/* Q44. A queue table anti-pattern is using a plain table as a job queue where many workers SELECT and lock the same "pending" rows; without care, workers block each other heavily on the same rows/index pages under high concurrency. */
/* Q45. SKIP LOCKED lets a worker's SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED simply skip rows another worker already has locked, instead of waiting - so many workers can pull different queue rows concurrently without blocking each other. */
/* Q46. DDL takes ACCESS EXCLUSIVE because changing a table's structure isn't safely visible mid-change to concurrent readers/writers; online schema changes are done safely via tools/patterns that make changes in small, mostly-non-blocking steps (e.g. adding a nullable column, backfilling, then adding constraints separately). */
/* Q47. pg_locks shows every lock currently held/waited-on in the system; pg_stat_activity shows what each active session/query is doing - joined together, they let you find exactly which query is blocking which other query. */
/* Q48. Each SAVEPOINT starts a nested subtransaction with its own sub-XID; changes made after it are only visible within that subtransaction until the outer transaction commits, and rolling back to the savepoint discards just that subtransaction's changes while keeping earlier ones. */
/* Q49. A transaction left open for a very long time prevents autovacuum from advancing the "oldest needed" transaction ID horizon, risking the whole database creeping toward the 2-billion transaction wraparound limit if not caught in time. */
/* Q50. Two-phase commit adds real coordination overhead and failure-mode complexity (an orphaned "prepared" transaction can hold locks indefinitely) that's only worth it when truly coordinating atomic writes across separate database systems, which most single-database apps never need. */

/* Q51. Header (page metadata), line pointers (an array of offsets to each tuple), tuples (the actual row data, growing from the end backward), and free space (special) in between - PostgreSQL's standard 8KB heap page layout. */
/* Q52. FILLFACTOR reserves free space in each page (e.g. leaving 20-30% empty) so future UPDATEs can write a new row version in the same page (enabling HOT updates); tune it down on tables with frequent updates to non-indexed columns. */
/* Q53. TOAST (The Oversized-Attribute Storage Technique) moves large column values out-of-line into a separate table once a row exceeds ~2KB; storage modes are PLAIN (never toast), MAIN (compress in-line first), EXTENDED (compress and/or move out-of-line), EXTERNAL (store out-of-line uncompressed). */
/* Q54. EXTERNAL stores the value out-of-line uncompressed (fast substring access, more disk space); EXTENDED tries compression first before moving out-of-line (less space, more CPU to decompress on read). */
/* Q55. The tuple header stores MVCC/system metadata (xmin, xmax, ctid, null bitmap, etc.) needed for every row regardless of its actual data, which is why even a tiny row carries meaningful fixed overhead. */
/* Q56. PostgreSQL aligns column values in memory/disk to their type's natural boundary, so ordering columns from largest/most-aligned type to smallest can reduce wasted padding bytes per row versus a poorly ordered column list. */
/* Q57. pg_catalog is PostgreSQL's own internal, PostgreSQL-specific system catalog (fast, complete, but non-portable); information_schema is the SQL-standard view layer on top of it that's portable across databases but sometimes slower/less complete. */
/* Q58. reltuples is only updated by ANALYZE/VACUUM (and a few DDL operations), not on every INSERT/DELETE, so between statistics refreshes it can drift from the true row count. */
/* Q59. pg_attribute is PostgreSQL's low-level internal catalog of every column (including dropped/system columns) with raw type OIDs; information_schema.columns is a friendlier, standardized view derived from it, omitting internal-only details. */
/* Q60. System columns are hidden per-row metadata (ctid = physical location, xmin/xmax = creating/deleting transaction IDs, tableoid = source table); you query them for debugging MVCC visibility, deduplication, or locating a row's physical page. */
/* Q61. Yes conceptually - a partitioned table itself has no storage; each partition is its own regular heap table, and the parent is just a routing/constraint layer directing rows to the correct partition heap. */
/* Q62. LIST partitions by explicit discrete values (e.g. by region name); RANGE partitions by a continuous range (e.g. sales.orders by order_date month); HASH partitions by a hash of a key for even distribution (e.g. customers by hashed customer_id) when no natural range/list split exists. */
/* Q63. Partition pruning is the planner skipping partitions that can't possibly match the query's WHERE clause; it decides using the partition key's constraints at plan time (or run time for parameterized queries), based on the filter conditions provided. */
/* Q64. A single global index spanning all partitions would need cross-partition coordination that conflicts with partitions' independence (each is its own heap/index set); the workaround is creating the same index on each partition individually (or letting PostgreSQL do so automatically via a partitioned index definition). */
/* Q65. A TABLESPACE maps a location to a physical disk path, letting you place specific tables/indexes on different storage; most setups don't need one because a single fast disk/volume already serves the whole database well enough. */
/* Q66. A foreign table (via a Foreign Data Wrapper) lets PostgreSQL query an external data source as if it were a local table; RetailMart could use postgres_fdw to query an archived "cold" orders database on another server without manually exporting/importing data. */
/* Q67. A unique constraint is a named integrity rule enforced (internally) via a unique index; functionally they behave the same, but a unique index alone (without a constraint) can't be referenced by a foreign key and isn't listed as a formal table constraint. */
/* Q68. An exclusion constraint generalizes uniqueness to "no two rows may overlap under this operator" - e.g. preventing two bookings for the same room from having overlapping date ranges, using EXCLUDE USING gist (room_id WITH =, daterange WITH &&). */
/* Q69. Partial indexes only index rows matching a WHERE condition; RetailMart cases: indexing sales.orders WHERE status = 'pending' (a small hot subset of a huge table) or support.tickets WHERE resolved = false - both index a tiny fraction of the full table. */
/* Q70. An expression index indexes the result of a function/expression rather than a raw column; use it when queries filter on lower(email) (case-insensitive lookup) or date_trunc('day', ts) (daily bucketing), since a plain index on the raw column can't be used for those filters. */
/* Q71. B-tree: equality/range on orders.order_date; Hash: pure equality lookup on a UUID; GiST: geometric/range types like booking date ranges; GIN: JSONB/array containment on products.promotions; BRIN: huge append-only time-ordered tables like web_events.page_views. */
/* Q72. BRIN stores tiny per-block summaries (min/max) which works great for naturally ordered time-series data at a fraction of a B-tree's size; its tradeoff is poor performance if the data isn't well-correlated with physical insertion order (e.g. after heavy updates/reordering). */
/* Q73. CLUSTER physically reorders a table's rows on disk to match a chosen index's order (improving range-scan locality); it isn't maintained automatically because it requires an exclusive table lock and a full rewrite, so PostgreSQL leaves it as a manual, occasional maintenance operation. */
/* Q74. TRUNCATE writes minimal WAL (just deallocates pages) and only needs a brief exclusive lock; DROP TABLE similarly writes little WAL but also removes all catalog entries and dependent objects, needing an exclusive lock for the whole drop operation. */
/* Q75. CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY builds the index in multiple passes without holding a long exclusive lock, writing extra WAL and doing two full table scans (plus waiting out other transactions) instead of one - trading speed for avoiding blocking concurrent writes. */

/* Q76. Physical replication for a full-database HA standby that must fail over instantly (e.g. disaster recovery); logical replication for selectively syncing specific tables to another system, or for zero-downtime major-version upgrades, or for feeding a different downstream schema. */
/* Q77. A hot standby is a replica that accepts read-only queries while continuously applying WAL from the primary; it can run SELECT queries but cannot accept any writes (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE/DDL). */
/* Q78. Replica lag is the delay between a change committing on the primary and appearing on the standby, caused by network latency, replica I/O/CPU saturation, or long-running queries blocking WAL apply; monitor it via pg_stat_replication's write/flush/replay lag columns. */
/* Q79. synchronous_standby_names designates which standby(s) must confirm receipt of WAL before a primary's COMMIT returns; it trades higher write latency (waiting for the standby) for a stronger durability guarantee (zero data loss if the primary dies right after commit) versus async replication. */
/* Q80. On primary failure, a failover promotes a standby to become the new primary and redirects traffic to it; tools like Patroni or repmgr automate detecting the failure, electing/promoting the right standby, and reconfiguring the cluster/DNS automatically. */
/* Q81. Connection pooling reuses a small set of real database connections across many client requests instead of opening one per request; pgBouncer is essential at high concurrency because PostgreSQL's per-connection memory/process cost makes thousands of direct connections expensive or impossible. */
/* Q82. Session pooling assigns one backend connection per client session for its whole duration; transaction pooling shares backends across clients per-transaction (more efficient, but breaks session-level features); statement pooling shares even more aggressively, per statement (most restrictive). */
/* Q83. Prepared statements are tied to one specific backend session, but transaction pooling can hand a client a different backend connection on each transaction, so a previously prepared statement may not exist there; the fix is disabling prepared statements or using pgBouncer's newer prepared-statement support. */
/* Q84. A thundering herd is when a large burst of requests (e.g. after a cache expiry during a flash sale) all hit the database/pool simultaneously, exhausting the connection pool at once - RetailMart's checkout traffic spiking right when a promotions cache entry expires is a realistic example. */
/* Q85. Vertical scaling (bigger server) is simpler and wins until you hit hardware/cost limits; horizontal scaling (sharding) wins once a single server truly can't hold the write throughput or data volume needed, despite its added operational complexity. */
/* Q86. Citus shards tables across multiple PostgreSQL nodes based on a distribution column, transparently routing and parallelizing queries across those shards while still speaking standard SQL/Postgres wire protocol to clients. */
/* Q87. Read replicas offload read traffic from the primary; the read-after-write consistency trap is a user's own write not yet being visible on the replica they're immediately routed to read from, appearing as if their action "didn't save." */
/* Q88. Without a matching index (id DESC), the planner may need to scan backward across a large unindexed range or fail to use an efficient index-only scan, making a simple "last row" query surprisingly expensive on a huge table. */
/* Q89. The N+1 problem is running one query to get a list, then one additional query per item in a loop instead of a single JOIN; example: fetching sales.orders, then looping to query order_items separately for each order instead of one JOINed query. */
/* Q90. pg_dump takes a logical, portable backup (slower, easy to restore selectively) at a point in time; pg_basebackup takes a physical binary copy of the whole cluster (fast, full-cluster restore); WAL archiving continuously saves WAL segments enabling point-in-time recovery between base backups. */
/* Q91. PITR (Point-In-Time Recovery) restores a database to any specific moment by replaying archived WAL from a base backup up to that timestamp; it depends on having both a base backup and a continuous, unbroken WAL archive covering that period. */
/* Q92. pg_upgrade can often upgrade in-place (or via hard links) in minutes regardless of database size, while pg_dump/restore rewrites the entire database from scratch, taking time proportional to data size - pg_upgrade is chosen specifically to minimize downtime on large databases. */
/* Q93. A rolling upgrade using logical replication sets up the new-version database as a logical subscriber, lets it catch up while the old primary stays live, then cuts traffic over with minimal downtime - needed when a hard-cutover upgrade's downtime window is unacceptable. */
/* Q94. Identify and terminate the long-running transaction holding back VACUUM's cleanup horizon, then run VACUUM (or VACUUM FULL/pg_repack if severely bloated) during low traffic to reclaim the accumulated dead-tuple space. */
/* Q95. The "noisy neighbor" problem is another tenant/workload on shared managed-database infrastructure consuming disproportionate I/O/CPU and degrading your database's performance; detect it via unexplained latency spikes uncorrelated with your own query load, visible in cloud provider monitoring metrics. */
/* Q96. pg_repack and pg_squeeze rebuild a table/index online (with much shorter locking) to remove bloat, unlike VACUUM FULL's full exclusive lock; they're used when VACUUM FULL's downtime is unacceptable on a live production table. */
/* Q97. Index bloat is wasted space in an index from many dead/obsolete entries; measure it with pgstattuple or bloat-estimation queries, and REINDEX CONCURRENTLY when it's grown large enough to hurt performance, without blocking concurrent access. */
/* Q98. Very high insert/update/delete churn produces dead tuples faster than default autovacuum settings can clean them up; tune by lowering autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor/cost_limit thresholds or running more frequent targeted vacuums on that specific table. */
/* Q99. Row-Level Security lets PostgreSQL automatically filter which rows a given role can see/modify based on a policy expression; a real multi-tenant RetailMart case would apply a policy like "tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id')" so every query automatically scopes to one tenant. */
/* Q100. Partition high-volume tables (orders, order_items, page_views) by date, add read replicas for analytics/reporting load, move to connection pooling if not already, evaluate sharding/Citus once single-node write throughput is the bottleneck, and formalize a backup/PITR and HA (Patroni) strategy. */

/* ============================================================
   END OF ANSWER KEY - HARD LEVEL (100/100)
============================================================ */
